      <div>
                  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">When should you choose each?</h2>
                  <div class="space-y-3">
                    <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                      <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Work with a psychologist if:</h3>
                      <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                        <li>• You are experiencing emotional difficulties such as anxiety, stress, relationship problems or low self-confidence</li>
                        <li>• You want to understand and change your behavioural patterns</li>
                        <li>• You are trying to cope with trauma, loss or a period of transition</li>
                        <li>• You need psychological testing or a comprehensive assessment</li>
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                      <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">See a psychiatrist if:</h3>
                      <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                        <li>• A serious diagnosis such as major depression, bipolar disorder or psychosis is involved</li>
                        <li>• Medication is being considered or an existing prescription needs adjusting</li>
                        <li>• There is a risk of harm to yourself or others</li>
                        <li>• You have a history of psychiatric inpatient treatment</li>
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Can they work together?
    metin: Yes — and the best outcomes are often achieved this way. The psychiatrist manages the medication; the psychologist uses therapy to address thought patterns and behaviour. This "combined treatment" is strongly supported by research, particularly for moderate to severe [depression](/en/blog/what-is-depression.html) and [anxiety disorders](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html).
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What do other titles mean — counsellor, psychologist, coach?
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Counsellor / Psychological Counsellor
        metin: Typically holds a degree in counselling or guidance. Often works in educational settings and cannot make clinical diagnoses.
      - baslik: Psychologist
        metin: A licensed mental health professional with a degree in psychology (typically a master's or doctoral level). Conducts assessments and delivers psychotherapy; cannot prescribe medication.
      - baslik: Life Coach
        metin: Not a clinical title and is not equipped to treat mental health conditions. May be supportive for goal-setting and motivation.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: Still unsure?
    metin: When you are not sure where to start, speaking with a psychologist is a good first step. The psychologist can refer you to a psychiatrist if needed, but many situations can be addressed through therapy alone.
